{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"eed7b3d16d9ff18f","type":"text","text":"AP Deployment modes","x":-1640,"y":1344,"width":423,"height":50,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"9e010a45924c08c6","type":"text","text":"Three main wireless AP \n## deployment methods:\n- Autonomous\n- Lightweight\n- Cloud-based","x":-1640,"y":1394,"width":423,"height":246},
		{"id":"e1c6fcadf5dd704a","type":"text","text":"Autonomous APs (sometimes called a local MAC architecture)","x":-3833,"y":2496,"width":791,"height":66,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"16ebd6a3b9fbfbd6","type":"text","text":"## Main Idea\n- self contained systems that don't rely on a WLC\n- There should be a VLAN for each SSID the AP offers, as well as a VLAN for Network Device Management\n- Connect to the wired network with a trunk link\n- autonomous APs are configured individually\n\t- can be configured by console cable (CLI), telnet/SSH (CLI), or HTTP/HTTPS web connection (GUI)\n\t- An IP address for remote management should be configured\n\t- The RF parameters must be manually configured (transmit power, channel, etc.)\n\t- Security policies are handled invidually by each AP\n\t- QoS rules etc. are configured individually on each AP\n\t- There is no central monitoring or management of APs","x":-3834,"y":2562,"width":790,"height":394},
		{"id":"21de5d792659ef07","type":"text","text":"- Autonomous APs connect to the wired network with a trunk link\n\t- there might be two SSIDs and a trunk connection is needed because each SSID is mapped to a VLAN\n\t- but even if there is only one SSID, the connection should be a trunk because the management traffic used to remotely connect to the wireless APs, as well as the other devices, the switches, should be in a separate VLAN\n- It's best practice to keep management traffic separate fro regular data by putting it in a separate subnet and VLAN\n- It is bad practice to have each VLAN stretch across the entire network\n\t- large broadcast domains\n\t- spanning tree will disable links (modern design avoids spanning tree as much as possible)\n\t- adding/deleting VLANs is very labor-intensive\n- Autonomous APs can be used in small networks, but they are not viable in medium to large networks\n\t- Large networks can have thousands of APs\n- Autonomous APs can also function in the modes covered in the previous video: repeater, outdoor bridge, workgroup bridge","x":-3840,"y":2956,"width":794,"height":480},
		{"id":"519eb3bc62496760","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803171131.png","x":-3834,"y":3436,"width":788,"height":443},
		{"id":"7127e3a7ff4f856d","type":"text","text":"Lightweight APs (AKA Split MAC architecture)","x":-2142,"y":2471,"width":792,"height":59,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a040c35808cdd6ee","type":"text","text":"## Main Idea\n-  The functions of an AP can be split between the AP and a Wireless LAN Controller (WLC)\n- A trunk link is needed to connect the WLC to the wired network. In this case instead of the AP mapping each SSID to a VLAN and forming the border between the wired and wireless network, the WLC takes the role\n- Traffic from a wireless client is sent to the AP and then tunneled to the WLC. Then the WLC sends it over the wired network to the default gateway, which will forward it as appropriate. Even if the traffic is destined for a host associated with the same AP, it is first tunneled to the WLC, and then back and sent to the destination \n\n### Functions of the lightweight AP\n- Lightweight APs handle 'real-time' operations like transmitting/receiving RF traffic, encryption/decryption of traffic, sending out beacons/probes, etc.\n\n### Functions of the Wireless LAN Controlelr (WLC)\n- Other functions are carried out by a WLC, for exampls RF management, security/QoS management, client authentication, client association/roaming management, etc.\n\t\t- This is called split-MAC architecture\n- The WLC is also used to centrally configure the lightweight APs\n- The WLC can be located in the same subnet /VLAN as the lightweight APs it manages, or in a different subnet/VLAN\n- The WLC and the lightweight APs authenticate each otehr using digital certificates installed on each device (X.509 certificates).\n\t- This ensures that only authorized APs can join the network (so an attacker cant make their AP join the network)","x":-2142,"y":2530,"width":792,"height":741},
		{"id":"e4dc41d881a1c5aa","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803171106.png","x":-2142,"y":3271,"width":796,"height":448},
		{"id":"7f88ca40188d393b","type":"text","text":"\n- This is the default mode where the AP offers a BSS (more multiple BSSs) for clients to associate with.","x":-1217,"y":3244,"width":289,"height":287},
		{"id":"1d11e53033559b09","type":"text","text":" Monitor","x":-1217,"y":3554,"width":289,"height":75,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"b08315528f99add3","type":"text","text":"\n- The AP does not offer a BSS for clients. It is dedicated to receiving 802.11 frames to detect rogue devices. If a client is found to be a rogue device, an AP can send de-authentication messages to disassociate the rogue device from the AP","x":-1217,"y":3629,"width":289,"height":325},
		{"id":"7e758a1da5b75c79","type":"text","text":"- Like the autonomous AP's Outdoor Bridge, the lightweight AP can be a dedicated bridge between sites, for example over long distances. A mesh can be made between the access points","x":-1217,"y":4040,"width":289,"height":324},
		{"id":"da3496512b9c3f94","type":"text","text":"Bridge/Mesh","x":-1217,"y":3980,"width":289,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d4ec57936de54c2d","type":"text","text":"\n- Like a lightweight AP in local mode, it offers one or more BSSs for clients to associate with. However, flexConnect allows the AP to locally switch traffic between the wired and wireless networks if the tunnels to the WLC go down. ","x":-894,"y":3252,"width":295,"height":279},
		{"id":"f465baf1cf21eee7","type":"text","text":"Rogue Detector","x":-894,"y":3554,"width":295,"height":75,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"7fbcd3b6a2deb925","type":"text","text":"\n- The AP does not even use its radio. It listens to the traffic on the wired network only, but it receives a list of suspected rogue clients and AP MAC addresses from the WLC. By listening to ARP messages on the wired network and correlating it with the information it receives from the WLC, it can detect rogue devices.","x":-894,"y":3629,"width":295,"height":325},
		{"id":"4a42b8b08bfdf75b","type":"text","text":"\n- The AP does not offer a BSS for clients. It is dedicated to capturing 802.11 frames and sending them to a device running software such as wireshark.","x":-557,"y":3253,"width":299,"height":279},
		{"id":"5a7809f9bca6d9b0","type":"text","text":"SE-Connect (Spectrum Expert Connect)","x":-557,"y":3555,"width":299,"height":76,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"7f6cd1929a73a68c","type":"text","text":"\n- The AP does not offer a BSS for clients. It is dedicated to RF spectrum analysis on all channels. It can send information to software such as Cisco Spectrum Expert on a PC to collect and analyze the data","x":-557,"y":3631,"width":299,"height":324},
		{"id":"c07e4285217db009","type":"text","text":"Adds FlexConnect functionality to the Bridge/Mesh mode. Allows wireless access points to locally forward traffic even if connectivity to the WLC is lost.","x":-894,"y":4040,"width":299,"height":324},
		{"id":"97bfea5093882b2e","type":"text","text":"Flex plus bridge","x":-894,"y":3980,"width":299,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"011a969afa5ae2e1","type":"text","text":"Lightweight AP modes","x":-1217,"y":3081,"width":959,"height":50,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"42c05cd2d1135406","type":"text","text":"Local","x":-1217,"y":3180,"width":289,"height":64,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"7da4781e6969b577","type":"text","text":"FlexConnect","x":-894,"y":3180,"width":295,"height":72,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"832af0ff15cc475d","type":"text","text":"Sniffer","x":-557,"y":3181,"width":299,"height":72,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"bb59c20f1cfe26fa","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803173247.png","x":-1217,"y":2471,"width":453,"height":519},
		{"id":"35f389bb223af8c8","type":"text","text":"Benefits of a split MAC architecture","x":-764,"y":2471,"width":792,"height":59,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2bac8fd50b9ce76b","type":"text","text":"\n- Scalability\n\t- with a WLC (or multiple in very large networks) it's much simpler to build and support a network with  thousands of APs\n- Dynamic channel assignment\n\t- The WLC can automatically select which channel each AP should use\n- Transmit power optimization\n\t- The WLC can automatically set the appropriate transmit power for each AP.\n- Self-healing wireless coverage\n\t- When an AP stops functioning, the WLC can increase the transmit power of nearby APs to avoid coverage holes\n- Client Load Balancing\n\t- if a clint is in range of two APs, the WLC can associate the clent with the least-used AP, to balance the load among APs\n- Security/QoS management\n\t- Central management of security and QoS policies ensures consistency across the network","x":-764,"y":2530,"width":792,"height":460},
		{"id":"52da192ad268e1b2","type":"text","text":"Traffic Paths used by the two architectures","x":-3046,"y":4152,"width":796,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ada8524bf7df57be","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803172429.png","x":-3046,"y":4202,"width":796,"height":448},
		{"id":"4401750beeead8f7","type":"text","text":"- The lightweight APs have to direct all traffic to the WLC first before it is directed to its final destination.\n- The Autonomous AP only redirects traffic up the network when the address needed is higher up or outside the default gateway. However, if the address is within a LAN then it is distributed to the correct endhost by the AP","x":-3046,"y":4650,"width":796,"height":262},
		{"id":"10a07c650fb024f4","type":"text","text":"Cloud-Based AP","x":400,"y":2471,"width":480,"height":78,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"842b722373cd4806","type":"text","text":"- Architecture is in between autonomous AP and split-MAC architecture.\n\t- autnomous APs that are centrally managed in the cloud\n- Cisco Meraki is a popular cloud-based Wi-Fi solution\n- The Meraki dashboard can be used to configure APs, monitor the network, generate performance reports, etc.\n\t- Meraki also tells each AP which channel to use, what transmit power to use, etc.\n- However, data traffic is not sent to the cloud. It is sent directly to the wired network like when using autonomous APs\n\t- Only management/control traffic is sent to the cloud","x":400,"y":2549,"width":480,"height":441},
		{"id":"1e2a2e24b6b73fd4","type":"text","text":"802.11 Frame Format","x":-599,"y":632,"width":978,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"8379d96d7996ce8d","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803153902.png","x":-599,"y":692,"width":978,"height":550},
		{"id":"0041a6b17b4ef5d0","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803153309.png","x":-599,"y":1242,"width":978,"height":550},
		{"id":"8ff71951d1b6513f","type":"text","text":"802.11 Association Process","x":-2934,"y":1224,"width":792,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"5bb81163d839305d","type":"text","text":"- Access points bridge traffic between wireless stations and other devices\n- For a station to send traffic through the AP, it must be associated with the AP.\n- There are three 802.11 connection states:\n\t- not authenticated, not associated\n\t- authenticated, not associated\n\t- authenticated, and associated\n- The station must be authenticated and associated with the AP to  send traffic through it.","x":-2934,"y":1284,"width":792,"height":220},
		{"id":"a7aa3d96e676af99","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803154526.png","x":-2934,"y":1504,"width":791,"height":263},
		{"id":"b13390fd1d590a6a","type":"text","text":"802.11 Message Types","x":-1824,"y":520,"width":791,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3da0b0522fb1b400","type":"text","text":"- There are three 802.11 message types\n## Management\n- used to manage the BSS.\n\t- Beacon\n\t- Probe request, probe response\n\t- Authentication\n\t- Association request, association response\n## Control\n- used to control access to the medium (radio frequency). Assists with delivery of management and data frames.\n\t- RTS (Request to send)\n\t- CTS (Clear to send)\n\t- ACK\n## Data\n- Used to send actual data packets","x":-1824,"y":580,"width":791,"height":588},
		{"id":"697d3d3a4879fafb","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803182428.png","x":160,"y":3103,"width":480,"height":270},
		{"id":"b016280e69354105","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803182501.png","x":680,"y":3103,"width":480,"height":270},
		{"id":"f34c5f5d623de32c","type":"text","text":"Unified","x":-1816,"y":4938,"width":289,"height":50,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"e8631fb834cd74fb","type":"text","text":"- The WLC is a hardware appliance in a central location of the network\n- Supports about 6000 APs","x":-1816,"y":4987,"width":289,"height":192},
		{"id":"385c4f06b6df633e","type":"text","text":"Rogue Detector","x":-1493,"y":4938,"width":295,"height":50,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"ec2cb0a946535ba1","type":"text","text":"- The WLC is a VM running on a server, usually in a private cloud in a data center. This is not the same as the cloud-based AP architecture discussed previously\n- Supports about 3000 APs","x":-1493,"y":4987,"width":295,"height":193},
		{"id":"9945bfe9f3caff52","type":"text","text":"Embedded","x":-1156,"y":4939,"width":299,"height":51,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"e46503ac344a514d","type":"text","text":"- The WLC is integrated within a switch\n- Supports about 200 Aps","x":-1156,"y":4988,"width":299,"height":191},
		{"id":"f85b3bba627c5c8a","type":"text","text":"Mobility Express","x":-820,"y":4939,"width":299,"height":51,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"dd83707b6b0ccce6","type":"text","text":"- The WLC is integrated within an AP\n- Supports about 100 APs","x":-820,"y":4989,"width":299,"height":191},
		{"id":"0950dbc5924e3f20","type":"text","text":"WLC Deployments","x":-1401,"y":4680,"width":407,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ee01ae2e51af8239","type":"text","text":"- in a split-MAC architecture, there are four main WLC deployment models:","x":-1401,"y":4740,"width":407,"height":81},
		{"id":"bd11531b4bfb763c","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803183013.png","x":-2071,"y":5320,"width":400,"height":225},
		{"id":"7e97e2772b157915","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803183113.png","x":-1601,"y":5320,"width":400,"height":225},
		{"id":"9883128cb0f8ded8","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803183159.png","x":-1144,"y":5320,"width":400,"height":225},
		{"id":"a0e53c898c910a21","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260803183235.png","x":-670,"y":5320,"width":400,"height":225}
	],
